  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "The quadratic polynomial $P(y)$ has the double root ${y}_{1}={y}_{2}=3,$ and the coefficient of the quadratic term is $a=3.$ Find $P(y).$",
  "worked_solution": "Since our quadratic polynomial has the double root ${y}_{1}={y}_{2}=3,$ and the coefficient of the quadratic term is $a=3,$ we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(y-{y}_{1})^{2} & =3{(y-3)}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{(y-3)}^{2} & =3({y}^{2}-6y+9) \\\\  & =3{y}^{2}-18y+27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(y)=3{y}^{2}-18y+27.$",
